---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { RESERVED } from '../lib/facts';
---
<Base
  title="The format: perch"
  description="Inside a portable durable object file: the reserved _perch_ tables that hold code, history, KV and the alarm, and the data tables that belong to the object."
>
  <div class="wrap head stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">Format version 1 · api perch-v0</p>
    <h1>Inside a <code>.perch</code> file.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      An object file is an ordinary SQLite database. Open it with <code>sqlite3</code> and you can
      read all of it. Seven tables whose names start with <code>_perch_</code> belong to the runtime.
      Every other table belongs to the object.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap stack-lg">
      <div class="stack">
        <h2>The runtime’s half</h2>
        <p>
          Object code cannot read or write these. The runtime and the MCP tools can. Migrations
          cannot touch them either.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ol class="plain tables">
        {RESERVED.map((t) => (
          <li class="t hatch">
            <div class="t-head">
              <code class="name">{t.name}</code>
              <code class="cols">({t.columns})</code>
            </div>
            <p>{t.detail}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="stack">
        <h2>The object’s half</h2>
        <p>
          Every table that is not <code>_perch_</code> belongs to the object’s code, schema
          included. The class creates and changes its tables in its constructor, as a Durable
          Object does on Cloudflare; agents read them with <code>query_data</code>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack rules">
        <p><strong>Steps in files.</strong> Each schema change is a file, <code>migrations/0001_name.sql</code>, imported as text. The constructor runs the ones a small <code>schema_steps</code> table does not list yet.</p>
        <p><strong>Tried on a copy first.</strong> The preview is a copy of the live data, so a step that fails on real rows fails there.</p>
        <p><strong>All or nothing.</strong> A constructor that throws is rolled back, and a release whose code does not start on live is undone.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="stack">
        <h2>Code and history</h2>
        <p>
          A check-in is a manifest from path to blob hash, a parent, a message and a time. Its id is
          the SHA-256 of those. <code>_perch_meta.live_checkin</code> names the one that runs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Releasing writes one squashed check-in, moves <code>live_checkin</code> and starts the
          new code. Rolling back is a proposal that starts from an older check-in’s files, so it
          goes through the preview and the tests like any other change.
        </p>
      </div>
      <pre><span class="c">-- what runs</span>
SELECT value FROM _perch_meta WHERE key = 'live_checkin';

<span class="c">-- its files</span>
SELECT manifest FROM _perch_checkins WHERE id = :live;
<span class="c">-- {"{"}"src/index.ts":"9f2c…","src/uuid.ts":"41ab…"{"}"}</span>

SELECT data FROM _perch_blobs WHERE hash = '9f2c…';</pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="stack">
        <h2>The preview copy</h2>
        <p>
          <code>propose_change</code> copies the live file to <code>&lt;file&gt;.preview</code>, data
          included, applies the change there and runs the constructor. <code>read_object</code>,
          <code>call_object</code> and <code>query_data</code> with <code>target: "preview"</code>
          work on that copy. <code>release_change</code> is refused if live moved since the preview
          was made.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .head { padding-top: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem); padding-bottom: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem); }
  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 1.4rem + 4vw, 4rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
  }
  h1 code { font-size: 0.9em; background: none; padding: 0; color: var(--amber); }
  .tables {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 820px) { .tables { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }
  .t {
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--amber);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 0.9rem 1rem;
    background-color: var(--card);
  }
  .t-head { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.2rem 0.6rem; align-items: baseline; margin-bottom: 0.4rem; }
  .t-head code { background: none; padding: 0; }
  .name { color: var(--amber); font-weight: 700; }
  .cols { color: var(--ink-2); font-size: 0.78rem; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .t p { font-size: 0.92rem; }
  .rules p { padding-left: 1rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--green); }
  pre { font-size: 0.82rem; }
</style>
